{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"issue-1796-collection-gc-clock-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue #1796: Collection GC clock review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed implementation: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"39d68cd6"},{"type":"text","value":" (base "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ae2eb3fb"},{"type":"text","value":"). This record covers the\nclock correction and oracle added at that commit. The source report is\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1796","children":[{"type":"text","value":"issue #1796"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; it had no comments\nwhen read on 2026-09-29. The full claim ledger is retained in the task record."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract-and-bounded-result","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract and bounded result"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BaseCollectionConfig.gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" promises collection cleanup after the configured\nnumber of milliseconds without active subscribers. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"docs/guides/live-queries.md"},{"type":"text","value":"\ndocuments the live-query variant. Where "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"performance.now()"},{"type":"text","value":" is available and\nmonotonic, moving the wall clock must not move an already scheduled GC deadline.\nThe Collection's normal idle callback still follows the queue deadline."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The queue reads the current Performance clock for scheduling, timer selection,\nand processing. The previous "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Date.now()"},{"type":"text","value":" path remains the fallback where\nPerformance is unavailable. That fallback still inherits backward wall-clock\nadjustments. Some browsers pause Performance time through OS sleep despite the\nintended monotonic-clock contract; this review did not test suspend/resume and\ndoes not claim sleep-inclusive GC timing."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"red-and-green","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RED and GREEN"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With the new oracle on unmodified "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ae2eb3fb"},{"type":"text","value":", both the fixed-seed and random\ncampaign failed. The smallest shrink was a zero-delay appointment followed by\na one-millisecond backward wall-clock step: delivery occurred at elapsed time\none instead of zero. The public live-query fixture likewise retained one source\nsubscriber ten elapsed milliseconds after a one-millisecond GC appointment and\na 1,000-millisecond backward step. The source subscriber count was "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":"; the\ncontractual count was "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"39d68cd6"},{"type":"text","value":", the same two suites pass. The focused cleanup/restart and\nCollection lifecycle suites also pass: 98 tests across four files, no Vitest\ntype errors. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm --filter @tanstack/db build"},{"type":"text","value":" succeeds. The production change\nis five added lines and three removed lines: net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+2"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Tests and coverage\ndocumentation are counted separately."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-guide-audit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle guide audit"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The elapsed GC law comes from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BaseCollectionConfig.gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":"; this review limits the clock-step claim to runtimes with monotonic Performance time."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The reference stores elapsed appointments. It never imports the queue's deadline or reads its chosen clock."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The queue oracle names the contract, pure appointment model, action grammar, real queue driver, and callback-delivery comparison. The focused Collection test observes source subscription ownership."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed histories reconstruct backward delay and forward early-delivery risks. Removing wall-step actions loses both. Delays are integers 0–20 ms; steps are integers −1000–1000 ms. Invalid non-positive/non-finite Collection "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":" is excluded because lifecycle disables GC for it."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The driver calls the real "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CleanupQueue"},{"type":"text","value":"; the integration witness calls public Collection and live-query APIs and compares "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source.subscriberCount"},{"type":"text","value":" after elapsed GC."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unmodified "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ae2eb3fb"},{"type":"text","value":" is the hostile wall-clock design and fails at delivery/subscriber observations. Existing cancel, replacement, duplicate, and late controls remain green only when their deliberate faults are rejected."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same 100-run property executes with fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"20260913"},{"type":"text","value":" and a seedless random lane. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"oraclePropertyOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts seed and shrink path for direct replay."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No reference state was added: a wall step leaves elapsed appointments unchanged. A later elapsed advance distinguishes due from pending appointments."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"now"},{"type":"text","value":" is elapsed time; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wallClockOffset"},{"type":"text","value":" is a driver-only correction used to record elapsed callback time from Vitest's fake Date. Neither is a Collection lifecycle state."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle resets its singleton, timers, error spy, and real timers in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":". The public fixture explicitly cleans both Collections and restores timers. The recorded RED assertion was observed before cleanup and was not replaced by a cleanup failure."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public source-subscriber witness is a second formulation of the queue delivery law. It does not copy the queue's appointment model."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record ties the bounded evidence and limits to implementation commit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"39d68cd6"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits-and-next-owner","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits and next owner"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The queue oracle covers bounded appointment histories, including cancellation\nand replacement around wall steps. Callback-reentrant scheduling remains with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/cleanup-queue.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" as an omitted grammar axis.\nSuspend/resume behavior and runtimes without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"performance.now()"},{"type":"text","value":" also remain\nwith that owner. The focused live-query test covers one eager source, one live\nquery, and source subscription release after GC; it does not prove React render\nor unmount scheduling."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"follow-up-timer-clock-replacement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow-up: timer clock replacement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed PR head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1c2d53a0"},{"type":"text","value":". Verified repair: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d0f3ca00"},{"type":"text","value":". The original queue\ncaptured a Vitest fake "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Performance"},{"type":"text","value":" object in its singleton. After the test\nrestored real timers and installed fresh fake timers, a later GC appointment\nread the stale object's time while the current fake timer advanced. The focused\ntwo-installation test failed on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1c2d53a0"},{"type":"text","value":": its second callback was called zero\ntimes after its one-millisecond delay. The full reproduction was 12 failures in\nthree existing test files, including two 10,000-timer loops and ten collections\nthat remained ready or in error past their GC deadline."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d0f3ca00"},{"type":"text","value":" reads the currently installed Performance object at each queue clock\nread. The same focused test, all 12 previously failing tests, and the adjacent\nGC/live-query tests passed: 262 tests across ten files. The production diff\nagainst "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ae2eb3fb"},{"type":"text","value":" is now net "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"+1"}]},{"type":"text","value":" line. The replacement witness only swaps\nthe global clock after the previous appointment has drained. Replacing the\nglobal clock while an appointment remains pending is outside this tested\ncontract; normal runtimes keep one Performance clock for that interval."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"issue-1796-collection-gc-clock-review","text":"Issue #1796: Collection GC clock review","level":1},{"id":"contract-and-bounded-result","text":"Contract and bounded result","level":2},{"id":"red-and-green","text":"RED and GREEN","level":2},{"id":"oracle-guide-audit","text":"Oracle guide audit","level":2},{"id":"limits-and-next-owner","text":"Limits and next owner","level":2},{"id":"follow-up-timer-clock-replacement","text":"Follow-up: timer clock replacement","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Issue #1796: Collection GC clock review\n\nReviewed implementation: `39d68cd6` (base `ae2eb3fb`). This record covers the\nclock correction and oracle added at that commit. The source report is\n[issue #1796](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1796); it had no comments\nwhen read on 2026-09-29. The full claim ledger is retained in the task record.\n\n## Contract and bounded result\n\n`BaseCollectionConfig.gcTime` promises collection cleanup after the configured\nnumber of milliseconds without active subscribers. `docs/guides/live-queries.md`\ndocuments the live-query variant. Where `performance.now()` is available and\nmonotonic, moving the wall clock must not move an already scheduled GC deadline.\nThe Collection's normal idle callback still follows the queue deadline.\n\nThe queue reads the current Performance clock for scheduling, timer selection,\nand processing. The previous `Date.now()` path remains the fallback where\nPerformance is unavailable. That fallback still inherits backward wall-clock\nadjustments. Some browsers pause Performance time through OS sleep despite the\nintended monotonic-clock contract; this review did not test suspend/resume and\ndoes not claim sleep-inclusive GC timing.\n\n## RED and GREEN\n\nWith the new oracle on unmodified `ae2eb3fb`, both the fixed-seed and random\ncampaign failed. The smallest shrink was a zero-delay appointment followed by\na one-millisecond backward wall-clock step: delivery occurred at elapsed time\none instead of zero. The public live-query fixture likewise retained one source\nsubscriber ten elapsed milliseconds after a one-millisecond GC appointment and\na 1,000-millisecond backward step. The source subscriber count was `1`; the\ncontractual count was `0`.\n\nAt `39d68cd6`, the same two suites pass. The focused cleanup/restart and\nCollection lifecycle suites also pass: 98 tests across four files, no Vitest\ntype errors. `pnpm --filter @tanstack/db build` succeeds. The production change\nis five added lines and three removed lines: net **+2**. Tests and coverage\ndocumentation are counted separately.\n\n## Oracle guide audit\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The elapsed GC law comes from `BaseCollectionConfig.gcTime`; this review limits the clock-step claim to runtimes with monotonic Performance time. |\n| ORC-002 | The reference stores elapsed appointments. It never imports the queue's deadline or reads its chosen clock. |\n| ORC-003 | The queue oracle names the contract, pure appointment model, action grammar, real queue driver, and callback-delivery comparison. The focused Collection test observes source subscription ownership. |\n| ORC-004 | Fixed histories reconstruct backward delay and forward early-delivery risks. Removing wall-step actions loses both. Delays are integers 0–20 ms; steps are integers −1000–1000 ms. Invalid non-positive/non-finite Collection `gcTime` is excluded because lifecycle disables GC for it. |\n| ORC-005 | The driver calls the real `CleanupQueue`; the integration witness calls public Collection and live-query APIs and compares `source.subscriberCount` after elapsed GC. |\n| ORC-006 | Unmodified `ae2eb3fb` is the hostile wall-clock design and fails at delivery/subscriber observations. Existing cancel, replacement, duplicate, and late controls remain green only when their deliberate faults are rejected. |\n| ORC-007 | The same 100-run property executes with fixed seed `20260913` and a seedless random lane. `oraclePropertyOptions` accepts seed and shrink path for direct replay. |\n| ORC-008 | No reference state was added: a wall step leaves elapsed appointments unchanged. A later elapsed advance distinguishes due from pending appointments. |\n| ORC-009 | Model `now` is elapsed time; `wallClockOffset` is a driver-only correction used to record elapsed callback time from Vitest's fake Date. Neither is a Collection lifecycle state. |\n| ORC-010 | The oracle resets its singleton, timers, error spy, and real timers in `finally`. The public fixture explicitly cleans both Collections and restores timers. The recorded RED assertion was observed before cleanup and was not replaced by a cleanup failure. |\n| ORC-011 | The public source-subscriber witness is a second formulation of the queue delivery law. It does not copy the queue's appointment model. |\n| ORC-012 | This record ties the bounded evidence and limits to implementation commit `39d68cd6`. |\n\n## Limits and next owner\n\nThe queue oracle covers bounded appointment histories, including cancellation\nand replacement around wall steps. Callback-reentrant scheduling remains with\n`packages/db/tests/cleanup-queue.property.test.ts` as an omitted grammar axis.\nSuspend/resume behavior and runtimes without `performance.now()` also remain\nwith that owner. The focused live-query test covers one eager source, one live\nquery, and source subscription release after GC; it does not prove React render\nor unmount scheduling.\n\n## Follow-up: timer clock replacement\n\nReviewed PR head: `1c2d53a0`. Verified repair: `d0f3ca00`. The original queue\ncaptured a Vitest fake `Performance` object in its singleton. After the test\nrestored real timers and installed fresh fake timers, a later GC appointment\nread the stale object's time while the current fake timer advanced. The focused\ntwo-installation test failed on `1c2d53a0`: its second callback was called zero\ntimes after its one-millisecond delay. The full reproduction was 12 failures in\nthree existing test files, including two 10,000-timer loops and ten collections\nthat remained ready or in error past their GC deadline.\n\n`d0f3ca00` reads the currently installed Performance object at each queue clock\nread. The same focused test, all 12 previously failing tests, and the adjacent\nGC/live-query tests passed: 262 tests across ten files. The production diff\nagainst `ae2eb3fb` is now net **+1** line. The replacement witness only swaps\nthe global clock after the previous appointment has drained. Replacing the\nglobal clock while an appointment remains pending is outside this tested\ncontract; normal runtimes keep one Performance clock for that interval.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Issue #1796: Collection GC clock review\n\nReviewed implementation: `39d68cd6` (base `ae2eb3fb`). This record covers the\nclock correction and oracle added at that commit. The source report is\n[issue #1796](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1796); it had no comments\nwhen read on 2026-09-29. The full claim ledger is retained in the task record.\n\n## Contract and bounded result\n\n`BaseCollectionConfig.gcTime` promises collection cleanup after the configured\nnumber of milliseconds without active subscribers. `docs/guides/live-queries.md`\ndocuments the live-query variant. Where `performance.now()` is available and\nmonotonic, moving the wall clock must not move an already scheduled GC deadline.\nThe Collection's normal idle callback still follows the queue deadline.\n\nThe queue reads the current Performance clock for scheduling, timer selection,\nand processing. The previous `Date.now()` path remains the fallback where\nPerformance is unavailable. That fallback still inherits backward wall-clock\nadjustments. Some browsers pause Performance time through OS sleep despite the\nintended monotonic-clock contract; this review did not test suspend/resume and\ndoes not claim sleep-inclusive GC timing.\n\n## RED and GREEN\n\nWith the new oracle on unmodified `ae2eb3fb`, both the fixed-seed and random\ncampaign failed. The smallest shrink was a zero-delay appointment followed by\na one-millisecond backward wall-clock step: delivery occurred at elapsed time\none instead of zero. The public live-query fixture likewise retained one source\nsubscriber ten elapsed milliseconds after a one-millisecond GC appointment and\na 1,000-millisecond backward step. The source subscriber count was `1`; the\ncontractual count was `0`.\n\nAt `39d68cd6`, the same two suites pass. The focused cleanup/restart and\nCollection lifecycle suites also pass: 98 tests across four files, no Vitest\ntype errors. `pnpm --filter @tanstack/db build` succeeds. The production change\nis five added lines and three removed lines: net **+2**. Tests and coverage\ndocumentation are counted separately.\n\n## Oracle guide audit\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The elapsed GC law comes from `BaseCollectionConfig.gcTime`; this review limits the clock-step claim to runtimes with monotonic Performance time. |\n| ORC-002 | The reference stores elapsed appointments. It never imports the queue's deadline or reads its chosen clock. |\n| ORC-003 | The queue oracle names the contract, pure appointment model, action grammar, real queue driver, and callback-delivery comparison. The focused Collection test observes source subscription ownership. |\n| ORC-004 | Fixed histories reconstruct backward delay and forward early-delivery risks. Removing wall-step actions loses both. Delays are integers 0–20 ms; steps are integers −1000–1000 ms. Invalid non-positive/non-finite Collection `gcTime` is excluded because lifecycle disables GC for it. |\n| ORC-005 | The driver calls the real `CleanupQueue`; the integration witness calls public Collection and live-query APIs and compares `source.subscriberCount` after elapsed GC. |\n| ORC-006 | Unmodified `ae2eb3fb` is the hostile wall-clock design and fails at delivery/subscriber observations. Existing cancel, replacement, duplicate, and late controls remain green only when their deliberate faults are rejected. |\n| ORC-007 | The same 100-run property executes with fixed seed `20260913` and a seedless random lane. `oraclePropertyOptions` accepts seed and shrink path for direct replay. |\n| ORC-008 | No reference state was added: a wall step leaves elapsed appointments unchanged. A later elapsed advance distinguishes due from pending appointments. |\n| ORC-009 | Model `now` is elapsed time; `wallClockOffset` is a driver-only correction used to record elapsed callback time from Vitest's fake Date. Neither is a Collection lifecycle state. |\n| ORC-010 | The oracle resets its singleton, timers, error spy, and real timers in `finally`. The public fixture explicitly cleans both Collections and restores timers. The recorded RED assertion was observed before cleanup and was not replaced by a cleanup failure. |\n| ORC-011 | The public source-subscriber witness is a second formulation of the queue delivery law. It does not copy the queue's appointment model. |\n| ORC-012 | This record ties the bounded evidence and limits to implementation commit `39d68cd6`. |\n\n## Limits and next owner\n\nThe queue oracle covers bounded appointment histories, including cancellation\nand replacement around wall steps. Callback-reentrant scheduling remains with\n`packages/db/tests/cleanup-queue.property.test.ts` as an omitted grammar axis.\nSuspend/resume behavior and runtimes without `performance.now()` also remain\nwith that owner. The focused live-query test covers one eager source, one live\nquery, and source subscription release after GC; it does not prove React render\nor unmount scheduling.\n\n## Follow-up: timer clock replacement\n\nReviewed PR head: `1c2d53a0`. Verified repair: `d0f3ca00`. The original queue\ncaptured a Vitest fake `Performance` object in its singleton. After the test\nrestored real timers and installed fresh fake timers, a later GC appointment\nread the stale object's time while the current fake timer advanced. The focused\ntwo-installation test failed on `1c2d53a0`: its second callback was called zero\ntimes after its one-millisecond delay. The full reproduction was 12 failures in\nthree existing test files, including two 10,000-timer loops and ten collections\nthat remained ready or in error past their GC deadline.\n\n`d0f3ca00` reads the currently installed Performance object at each queue clock\nread. The same focused test, all 12 previously failing tests, and the adjacent\nGC/live-query tests passed: 262 tests across ten files. The production diff\nagainst `ae2eb3fb` is now net **+1** line. The replacement witness only swaps\nthe global clock after the previous appointment has drained. Replacing the\nglobal clock while an appointment remains pending is outside this tested\ncontract; normal runtimes keep one Performance clock for that interval.\n"}
